WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.860
Astrocytes clear potassium and glutamate.

2
00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:08.860
Do they also send fast signals of their own?

3
00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:11.173
Start with what is settled.

4
00:00:12.213 --> 00:00:15.573
Glutamate follows the same rule as potassium, even more sharply.

5
00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:20.567
Between releases, transporters keep outside glutamate very low.

6
00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:24.347
So each release makes a large, brief rise near the synapse.

7
00:00:25.317 --> 00:00:31.377
Most of the glutamate transporter GLT-1 sits in astrocytes, with a smaller pool in

8
00:00:31.377 --> 00:00:32.377
terminals.

9
00:00:32.817 --> 00:00:37.657
Deleting GLT-1 from astrocytes in mice caused seizures and early death.

10
00:00:38.617 --> 00:00:39.997
Now the open question.

11
00:00:40.957 --> 00:00:44.777
Do astrocytes also release glutamate that acts quickly on synapses?

12
00:00:45.777 --> 00:00:47.277
This is called gliotransmission.

13
00:00:48.267 --> 00:00:50.187
Astrocytes show calcium signals.

14
00:00:51.127 --> 00:00:55.347
In principle, a calcium rise could trigger release, as it does in a terminal.

15
00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:02.340
One test deleted a receptor that releases stored calcium, the IP3 receptor type 2.

16
00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:05.200
Hippocampal transmission did not change.

17
00:01:06.183 --> 00:01:10.123
Does that prove astrocytes never release glutamate onto synapses?

18
00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:22.027
No. In adult mice, astrocytes lacking that receptor still showed calcium

19
00:01:22.027 --> 00:01:24.447
fluctuations, mostly in their processes.

20
00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:30.250
So the null result cannot exclude effects driven by the calcium that remains.

21
00:01:31.383 --> 00:01:36.223
A later study found a subgroup of adult mouse hippocampal astrocytes with

22
00:01:36.223 --> 00:01:37.743
synaptic-like release machinery.

23
00:01:38.833 --> 00:01:43.773
Deleting a vesicular glutamate transporter from astrocytes suppressed the glutamate

24
00:01:43.773 --> 00:01:45.793
hotspots linked to that subgroup.

25
00:01:46.767 --> 00:01:50.407
That is positive evidence, but in one subgroup and one species.

26
00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:53.527
Neither study refutes the other.

27
00:01:54.427 --> 00:01:57.507
They used different manipulations and asked different questions.

28
00:01:58.617 --> 00:01:59.897
So keep two lists.

29
00:02:00.737 --> 00:02:03.917
That astrocytes clear potassium and glutamate is settled.

30
00:02:04.917 --> 00:02:07.157
Fast signalling by astrocytes is still argued.

31
00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:12.967
Your design problem is to test it in one group of adult mouse astrocytes.
